Abstract

We propose a relational-geometric framework in which spacetime is not a fundamental continuous manifold but a discrete topological network of phase-related nodes. The primordial state is a Harmonic Silence |0\rangle of perfect phase cancellation, \hat H|0\rangle=0, possessing an infinite latent potential (the capacity of the network to generate infinitely many phase configurations) but no actual distinction. A minimal angular perturbation \hat P(\theta)=\cos\theta breaks the symmetry and generates the first topological integer. The network cannot remain in equilibrium: an irreducible Information Debt \mathfrak D=ABC-2abc=1.5 appears and acts as the ontological engine of the cosmos. In this framework, particles are stable topological defects of the network rather than inserted "balls." The electron corresponds to a 19-node cluster in hexagonal close packing (1+6+12); the proton to a 57-node hard-lock (57=3×19). Spin 1/2 emerges from topological torsion and the angular deficit of the cluster; mass arises from confined Information Debt and topological friction \eta=\pi/57. Gravity is not a fundamental force but the elastic response of the network to mass; the strong interaction is a manifestation of local torsion. The model yields quantitative, falsifiable predictions: E=mc^2 emerges as a geometric consistency condition; the proton/electron mass ratio m_p/m_e\approx1836.15 is derived from the baryonic knot factor including the QCD trace anomaly (\beta_0=9) and topological packing, with an error of order 0.008%; and the doubly charmed baryon \Xi_{cc}^{++} is predicted near 3620\ \mathrm{MeV} once topological saturation is included, in close agreement with the LHCb value 3621.4\ \mathrm{MeV}. In the current cosmological epoch, the Information Debt behaves as an effective cosmological constant (w\approx -1), yielding an age of the universe of 13.76\ \mathrm{Gyr}, consistent with Planck. We present Relational Geometry (RG) as a geometric and falsifiable research program: every successful Standard Model equation should be re-derivable as a continuum limit of the discrete network. Gravity and the strong force, mass and spin, cosmic expansion and dark matter should cease to be hand-tuned parameters and become consequences of one and the same relational topology.

Keywords: gravedad cuántica; geometría relacional; red topológica; masa; espín; QCD; cosmología; predicciones falsables.

